#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167332 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x+2{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x(1+2x)}{x(1+x)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167541 :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171710 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A}}} \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z) = z-y$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: -v, \\: v \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v) \\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where $u \\geq 0.$ What is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4\\sqrt{2}uv\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171711 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} (x-y)\\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by the equations\n\n$$\nx = u+v, \\qquad \ny = u-v, \\qquad \nz = v\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x-y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171856 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are connected?\n\n![](images/ef/ef9e1dfd559c1e8818f14e100e07ab36ca167a4bd2ddf970bf3f5ef325103d64.png)" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, only regions I, II, and III satisfy this definition.\n\nRegion IV does not satisfy this definition. Note that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/d4/d4d61cf87f2c62c5cf4727384d6dfcc05cada6b23025dd6dd674130eaf64dd45.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171857 :question/problem "Which of the regions shown below are simply connected?\n\n![](images/b8/b8e630b749cbfee41d73a620ffd284f3216bb8a43affdc26543f1eb57e4613c5.png)" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions: \n\n- Region I is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171860 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =  \\left\\langle ye^{xy},\\, xe^{xy}\\right\\rangle$ and the points $A(1,1)$ and $B(2,2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,\n$$ where $C_1$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^4-e,\n$$ where $C_2$ is the path along along the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = (1+xy)e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\cdot 2}-{e}^{1\\cdot 1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf F$ is conservative and $C_2$ has the same initial and terminal points as $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = e^{4}-e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171889 :question/problem "Draw a diagram that represents the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n at the points $(-2,1), (-1,-2),$ and $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "To sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2xy \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} \\,\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,1) & =-2(-2)(1)\\,i+(-2)^{2}\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ F(-1,-2) & =-2(-1)(-2)\\,i+(-1)^{2}\\,j=-4\\,i+j \\\\ F(1,-1) & =-2(1)(-1)\\,i+{1}^{2}\\,j=2\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/cf/cf51cba8548d1f342a9dd5c06b533782feb955fca4b90adfac5b6988d7e2e470.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171890 :question/problem "![](images/29/2966d95bee0326ed5bf5a5fec9313d15bacd86334f9e9d144ff366bee63d9de3.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}$\n\n- $\\mathbf H(x,y) = -x \\, \\mathbf{i}$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf j$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $\\mathbf i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y) = 2 \\, \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{H}(x,y) = -x\\, \\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171891 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171892 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d4/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/24/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171893 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171894 :question/problem "![](images/de/de305db47bcfae58b75797e88ae4a7c7825174bd58d77991ba8dfb4c0b8a13cb.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?\n\n- $\\mathbf  F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf  K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\mathbf j + \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf G(x,y,z) = \\mathbf k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf H(x,y,z) = z\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf K(x,y,z) = -\\mathbf k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171897 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: y-x\\geq 0\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: x^2+y^2> 4\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y \\geq x.$ So, it's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not*  simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc $x^2+y^2 \\leq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171903 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-1,0,-1)-f(-2,1,0) \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2})-((-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171904 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 0)$ to $(1, 2)$ in $\\mathbb R^2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1, 2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf = 2y^3 - x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,2)-f(-2,0) \\\\  & =(2(2)^{3}-{1}^{2})-(2(0)^{3}-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =16-1+4 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171905 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171906 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172275 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i - 2yj$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "At the three indicated points, evaluate the components of $F(x,y)=\\langle x^2,-2y\\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(-2,1)&=\\langle4,-2\\rangle,\\\\\nF(2,3)&=\\langle4,-6\\rangle,\\\\\nF(0,-2)&=\\langle0,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matching diagram has a right-and-down arrow from each of the first two points and an upward arrow from $(0,-2)$, with the displayed component displacements."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172276 :question/problem "![](images/14/14a67562652c609deeaf6b7606cee130685e2c2b5382f1a5436cd4e441591b94.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "Every arrow points downward. Its horizontal component points left for $x<0$, vanishes on the $y$-axis, and points right for $x>0$. These features match\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=xi-j=\\langle x,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe constant vector fields would have the same direction everywhere, while $-xi+yj$ would point upward in the upper half-plane and inward horizontally."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172277 :question/problem "![](images/e3/e3614ea1954f36435eb1ab5f52c04d4fa3062ac337eccf76a5b47e4a03a64a1b.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{i}$ component) is negative for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{H}(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $\\mathbf{j}$ component) is positive for each vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{G}(x,y)=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172278 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$; $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172279 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172280 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ne \\pm x\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ne \\pm x\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172281 :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172282 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfc15b6202167211b7d6c0e8f6635dd9e571810d5296a209a4c317350604c4a4.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k},\\hspace{2em}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $x$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{i}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172283 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172284 :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a0a919af689e9c332ba6ceb3d606208dab9eabadef9b4871ac8e164843ebe88.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y,z)=-\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $\\mathbf{k}$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $\\mathbf{K}(x,y,z)=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172285 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $W(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $U(x,y,z)$ $=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172286 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,j\n$$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(-2,-1),$ and $(4,-4).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\,j:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-3))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{2}\\right)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-2,-1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-2))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2}\\right)\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-4) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (4))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-4)}{2}\\right)\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172291 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (1,1)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}2x-y\\ne 0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(1,1).$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=2x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172292 :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n1. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid 4x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 4}$\n2. ${(x, y) ∈ R^{2} \\mid y - x \\ne 0}$\n3. ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Region I is a filled ellipse, so it is convex and therefore simply connected. Region II is the plane with the entire line $y=x$ removed; it splits into the two disjoint half-planes $y>x$ and $y<x$, so it is not connected and hence not simply connected. Region III is the full plane, which is convex and simply connected. Thus I and III only are simply connected."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172299 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172300 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172301 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172302 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(1,-3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(4(1)-3(-3))-(4(0)-3(2)) \\\\  & =4+9+6 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172303 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172304 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\hspace{0.222em}3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/269e3c7f92cee66c78f3226e7c967eb51153503c598d2e4d1e4b56a33e646ac4.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3{x}^{5}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=15{x}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{5}+6xy]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(3{y}^{5}+6{y}^{2})-(-3{y}^{5}-6{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{y}^{5}+12{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{6}+4{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+4)-0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172305 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,{|}_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{4}-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
